S[1]=dict(lbl="What is employee green behavior?|Why is it the core of a sustainable organization?|Why call it a performance domain?|What forms can it take?|What does this review do?|What can you now answer?",cards=[
Q("How is employee green behavior defined, and why do the authors call it the core of a sustainable organization?",
 "This is the map reading for the whole theme: every later reading builds on its definition. An <b>organization</b> becomes <b>sustainable</b> (able to go on without using up what the future needs) only through what people in it do, so you need a precise idea of that behavior first.",
 ["The definition, read in parts","Why the behavior is the core of a sustainable organization","Why it counts as work performance, and the forms it takes","What this review sets out to do"]),
CARD("\"Scalable actions and behaviors that employees engage in that are linked with and contribute to or detract from environmental sustainability\" (Ones &amp; Dilchert, 2012).",
 "The review shortens <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"egb\">employee green behavior</b> to EGB (quoted on p. 467). Read it in parts:"+UL([("1","<b>Scalable:</b> it can be measured, by how often it happens or how well it is done."),("2","<b>Behaviors:</b> what the employee does and controls, not the outcomes of behavior."),("3","<b>Contribute to or detract from:</b> it can help or harm, judged by its impact on the natural environment.")]),
 "green behavior is countable behavior at work, for better or worse, not good intentions.",
 "why it matters so much."),
CARD("Because organizations cannot reach their environmental goals unless employees at different levels actually perform green behavior.",
 "Many organizations now put <b>environmental sustainability</b> goals in their strategy, next to economic and social goals: the <b>triple bottom line</b>. Once that is so, an employee's behavior can be judged by what it adds to or takes from those goals. <i>Our example:</i> a company pledges to halve its waste; that pledge only becomes real when people at the printer, in purchasing and in the board room act on it.",
 "the strategy is on paper; the behavior makes it real.",
 "how the authors classify this behavior."),
CARD("Because green behavior cuts across the established dimensions of work performance, held together by one goal: environmental sustainability.",
 "<b>Work performance</b> is goal-directed behavior that contributes to the organization's goals. Its established dimensions include task performance, citizenship (helping beyond your task), counterproductive behavior, team member performance and leadership performance. A <b>compound performance domain</b> is not one more box next to these; it runs through them, and the list is not comprehensive. Adaptive, proactive and innovative performance can also have an environmental impact.",
 "if green behavior is performance, everything known about the causes of performance applies to it (step 4).",
 "the forms it takes."),
CARD("Prescribed or discretionary, helpful or harmful, more relevant to some jobs, and present at every level of the hierarchy.",
 "<b>Prescribed:</b> creating sustainable products, delivering services in sustainable ways. <b>Discretionary</b> (left to your own choice): switching the computer off, or wasting paper. It can ask for initiative or creativity, such as voicing suggestions. It matters more for a sustainability officer than for a supermarket cashier, and runs from top managers' purchasing to team members' advocacy among coworkers. It differs from <b>pro-environmental behavior</b> outside work (behavior that harms the environment as little as possible, or even benefits it; Steg &amp; Vlek, 2009), because work brings its own motives, opportunities, constraints, norms, leaders and climate; but the two may share causes such as green attitudes, knowledge and habits.",
 "an exam option saying green behavior is only the good, voluntary things employees do is wrong.",
 "what the review adds."),
CARD("It reviews the field, builds one integrative model, and gives recommendations, looking at causes and consequences at several levels.",
 "A <b>review article</b> summarizes what a whole field has found. In 2012, Ones and Dilchert urged organizational psychologists to study the <b>microfoundations</b> of organizational sustainability: the individual behaviors on which the organization's results rest. Since then the field grew: four edited books, six systematic reviews and two meta-analyses. But most studies focused on individual antecedents (things that come before a behavior and may lead to it), such as attitudes, values, perceived norms and intentions. The authors argue for causes <b>and consequences</b> at five levels: individual, team, work context, organization, and society and culture.",
 "the review widens the lens from the person to everything around the person.",
 "check what you can now answer."),
RECAP("Employee green behavior is measurable behavior at work that helps or harms the environment; it is a performance domain and the core of a sustainable organization.",
 [("Definition?","Scalable behaviors that contribute to or detract from environmental sustainability (Ones & Dilchert, 2012)."),("Why the core?","Environmental goals are reached only through employees' behavior."),("Performance domain?","It cuts across all performance dimensions."),("Forms?","Prescribed or discretionary, helpful or harmful, at every level."),("What the review does?","Review, model, recommendations; causes and consequences at five levels.")],
 ["Which statement about employee green behavior is wrong? (\"only voluntary, helpful acts\")","Why do the authors call green behavior a compound performance domain?","How does employee green behavior differ from pro-environmental behavior outside work?"])],
 guides=G("Six moments from working life appear one at a time.","Decide: is it employee green behavior that <b>helps</b>, that <b>harms</b>, or <b>not green behavior</b> at all?","The picture sorts it; read the explanation that follows."))

S[2]=dict(lbl="What sits in the middle of the model?|How do individual causes work?|How do the surrounding levels work?|What does green behavior bring?|What can you now answer?",cards=[
Q("What is the authors' model of employee green behavior, and at which levels do its causes and consequences sit?",
 "Step 1 defined the behavior. Figure 1 places it in a map of causes and consequences. Every later step of this reading, and readings 2, 5, 7 and 8, fill in a part of this map. A <b>conceptual model</b> is a proposal built on research, not a tested result.",
 ["The core of the model: green behavior described three ways","How causes reach the behavior: direct and indirect paths","What the behavior brings, bottom-up"]),
CARD("Green behavior, described three ways: as a compound performance domain, as self-regulated behavior, and as something studied at several levels.",
 UL([("1","<b>A compound performance domain</b> with distinct dimensions (task-related, citizenship, proactive, innovative, counterproductive) that may have shared and unique causes; knowledge and skills may matter more for task-related forms, motivation more for discretionary ones."),("2","<b>Self-regulated:</b> employees steer it in a goal-directed way (step 4)."),("3","<b>Multilevel:</b> differences between persons, changes within a person over time, and a whole team's behavior added together.")]),
 "the middle of the map is richer than one behavior; it is a family of behaviors that people steer.",
 "how the causes reach it."),
CARD("Through three direct determinants (knowledge, skills, motivation) and indirect ones that work through them.",
 "<b>Direct determinants</b>: knowledge, skills and motivation. <b>Indirect</b> ones: stable individual differences (such as personality) and cognitive and affective states, which act through the direct ones. <i>Our example:</i> a green attitude only turns into recycling if it raises motivation, and the person also knows how and can do it.",
 "every individual cause has to pass through knowing, being able, or wanting.",
 "and the levels around the person?"),
CARD("Directly, by giving opportunities or putting up constraints, or indirectly, through the levels below them.",
 "The levels are the team, the work context (technology, equipment, job roles such as a sustainability officer, work design such as autonomy), the organization (strategies, initiatives, culture and climate), and society and culture (laws, technology, external stakeholders, cultural values). <i>Our example:</i> a law can work through an organization's strategy, which works through a team's norms, which work through one person's motivation. Leadership sits at the team level, next to coworker support, advocacy, norms and team climate.",
 "an exam option saying the organization influences employees only through its leaders is wrong.",
 "the other side of the map."),
CARD("Benefits that may build bottom-up from the individual to the team, the organization and society, possibly giving a competitive advantage and feeding back into more green behavior.",
 "For the <b>individual</b>: need satisfaction, identity development, self-efficacy, well-being, job performance, future intentions. <b>Team</b>: norms, climate, team environmental performance. <b>Work context</b>: green tasks and jobs, through job creation and green job crafting. <b>Organization</b>: environmental performance, attractiveness. <b>Society</b>: national and international sustainability goals. Outcomes can build step by step: members act green → green team climate → better team environmental performance → organizational goals met. Some individual outcomes may raise later green behavior: a <b>virtuous cycle</b>.",
 "the model mirrors itself: five levels in, five levels out.",
 "check what you can now answer."),
RECAP("Figure 1: five levels of causes → green behavior (a self-regulated, multilevel performance domain) → outcomes at five levels.",
 [("Middle?","Compound performance domain; self-regulated; multilevel."),("Individual causes?","Direct: knowledge, skills, motivation; indirect: traits and states."),("Surrounding levels?","Direct (opportunities, constraints) or indirect through lower levels."),("Outcomes?","Bottom-up at five levels; possible competitive advantage and virtuous cycle.")],
 ["Name the three direct determinants of green behavior","Where does the model place leadership? (team level)","Give one example of a bottom-up chain of outcomes"])],
 guides=G("Six events in and around a company appear one at a time.","<b>Tap the level</b> on the left where each one belongs: individual, team, work context, organization, or society and culture.","The level then shows what the model puts there, and what it brings."))

P31=("Why did researchers need a taxonomy?|What are the Green Five?|What is the sixth construct?|Why not split required and voluntary?|What can you now answer?",[
Q("Which five dimensions of employee green behavior does the taxonomy distinguish, how do they differ, and what is the sixth?",
 "A <b>taxonomy</b> is an ordered system of categories. With it, you can name any green behavior in a case, and see which kinds research has neglected.",
 ["Why a taxonomy was built","The Green Five","The sixth: counterproductive sustainability behaviors","Why the required/voluntary split is criticized"]),
CARD("Because early research focused mostly on single green acts, such as recycling paper; an overview of everything employees do was missing.",
 "Ones and Dilchert (2012) built the <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"green5\">Green Five</b> from thousands of critical incidents (real examples of good or bad behavior) collected in the United States and Europe. It is hierarchical: one overall pro-environmental performance factor, five broad dimensions, and 17 narrow subdimensions. Many constructs in the literature fit into one of the five.",
 "the taxonomy turns a pile of single acts into an ordered map.",
 "the five."),
CARD("Transforming, conserving, avoiding harm, influencing others and taking initiative.",
 UL([("🔁","<b>Transforming:</b> adapting work products and processes to make them more sustainable; doing required tasks in sustainable ways belongs here."),("💡","<b>Conserving:</b> preserving resources and avoiding waste (reducing use, recycling)."),("🛡️","<b>Avoiding harm:</b> reducing negative impact and mitigating damage, including environmental management activities."),("📣","<b>Influencing others:</b> promoting other people's green behavior (educating, training)."),("🚀","<b>Taking initiative:</b> proactive, entrepreneurial behaviors (initiating programs, lobbying, activism) involving personal risk and sacrifice.")])+"Each has a positive and a negative end (saving energy, wasting energy). Table 1 below gives subcategories and measures.",
 "to classify a behavior, ask what it is about: the process, resources, damage, other people, or initiative.",
 "what the five leave out."),
CARD("Counterproductive sustainability behaviors: actively harmful behaviors that risk or directly result in environmental harm (Dilchert, 2018).",
 "Dilchert noted that the taxonomies left out the more severe, actively harmful behaviors. Because sustainability is a strategic goal of many organizations, harming the environment detracts from that goal and violates the organization's legitimate interests: that makes it counterproductive. A related construct is \"nongreen behaviors\". The five plus this one are the \"green six\".",
 "wasting energy is the low end of conserving; the sixth is for more severe harm.",
 "a split the review rejects."),
CARD("Because it confounds the motive for a behavior with its content.",
 "Ones and colleagues criticized the split: it <b>confounds</b> (mixes up) why a behavior happens with what it is. Not printing e-mails is the same behavior whether a rule demands it or you choose it. So transforming, whose example measure is task-related, can also be discretionary.",
 "classify a green behavior by what it does, not by why the person does it.",
 "check what you can now answer."),
RECAP("The Green Five (transforming, conserving, avoiding harm, influencing others, taking initiative) plus counterproductive sustainability behaviors make the green six.",
 [("Why a taxonomy?","Research looked at single acts; Ones & Dilchert (2012) built an overview."),("Green Five?","Transforming, conserving, avoiding harm, influencing others, taking initiative."),("Sixth?","Counterproductive sustainability behaviors (Dilchert, 2018)."),("Required vs voluntary?","Confounds motive with content.")],
 ["Classify: an engineer redesigns a process to use less water (transforming)","Which statement is wrong? \"Wasting energy is a counterproductive sustainability behavior, outside the Green Five.\" (it is the low end of conserving)"])],
 G("Seven employees appear one at a time. Read what each one does.","<b>Tap the tile</b> that describes it.","Only then the tile gets its official name; read why."))
P32=("What are the three lines?|Why do the lines matter for research?|What can you now answer?",[
Q("Along which three lines can any green behavior be classified?",
 "The Green Five say what a behavior is about. The three lines (Francoeur and colleagues, 2021) say how it is carried out. <b>Continuous</b> means a line with two ends and everything in between, not two boxes.",
 ["The three lines","What they reveal about the research"]),
CARD("In-role or extra-role, direct or indirect, and low or high intensity.",
 UL([("1","<b>In-role</b> (part of your core tasks) or <b>extra-role</b> (what you do as an organizational citizen)."),("2","<b>Direct</b> (you act on the environment yourself) or <b>indirect</b> (you encourage others: coworkers, customers, supervisors, other departments)."),("3","<b>Low intensity</b> (less effort and risk, incremental) or <b>high intensity</b> (more effort and risk, radical). A related distinction: local versus wide impact.")])+"<i>Our example:</i> switching off the lights is extra-role and low intensity; redesigning a production process can be in-role and high intensity.",
 "an exam option saying extra-role and high intensity are the same is wrong: they are different lines.",
 "what the lines reveal."),
CARD("They show that questionnaires cover the lines unevenly, so what we know is mostly about voluntary, individual behavior.",
 "In the review of scales: 60% of items were about direct and 40% about indirect behavior; 41% were explicitly about voluntary behavior and only 5% about in-role behavior (55% did not say); 72% were about individual employees, 13% about leaders, 7% about pairs and 8% about collective behavior such as a team.",
 "the research picture is skewed; be careful generalizing to in-role or team behavior. Most scales were also built for office behavior (paper, printing, lights); production and customer service are hardly studied.",
 "check what you can now answer."),
RECAP("Three lines classify how a behavior is carried out; the measures lean heavily toward direct, voluntary, individual behavior.",
 [("Three lines?","In-role/extra-role; direct/indirect; low/high intensity."),("Why they matter?","Measures are uneven: mostly voluntary (41% vs 5% in-role) and individual (72%).")],
 ["Place: a manager persuades purchasing to buy recycled paper (indirect)","What share of scale items was about individual employees? (72%)"])],
 G("Six behaviors appear one at a time.","Each belongs at one end of one line. <b>Choose the end</b>.","The behavior lands on the line; read why."))
